  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "When Nagiyev finally wakes up in his office after sleeping off the mushrooms, he finds the restaurant empty except for Vika, who stays behind to talk with him.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Лена, прости меня, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты не видела моего слоника?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вика, ты хотела со мной поговорить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что это? Розыгрыш какой-то, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сволочи! А кого я накажу, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, ты мне хоть скажи, что происходит здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Единственный адекватный человек!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Который никогда не воровал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никогда не предавал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никогда мои кабачки не жарил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Красивая такая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, вам пора домой.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Лена, прости меня, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Lena, forgive me, please.' Notice how Russian expresses 'Lena' and 'please' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Ты не видела моего слоника?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Have you seen my little elephant?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'you' before landing on 'little elephant figurine'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Вика, ты хотела со мной поговорить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, did you want to talk with me?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'Vika' and finishing on 'to talk'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что это? Розыгрыш какой-то, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is this? Some kind of prank, huh?' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'what' and finishing on 'and'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сволочи! А кого я накажу, а?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Scoundrels! And who am I going to punish, huh?' Dmitry Nagiyev anchors the line on 'scoundrels', while 'will punish' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вика, ты мне хоть скажи, что происходит здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vika, at least tell me what is going on here?' Dmitry Nagiyev raises the pitch on 'here' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Единственный адекватный человек!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The only reasonable person!' In Russian, the idea of 'only' flows naturally into 'man' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Который никогда не воровал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who never stole.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'stole' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никогда не предавал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Never betrayed me.' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'never' and 'not' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никогда мои кабачки не жарил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Never secretly fried my zucchini.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'fried' to negate the statement.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Красивая такая.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So beautiful.' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'beautiful' and 'so' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Дмитрий Владимирович, вам пора домой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Dmitry Vladimirovich, it is time for you to go home.' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'home' without extra filler words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
